# Every generated artefact in this tree, and the definition that makes it.
#
#   <definition>   <output root>   <backend> [backend ...] [-only=<surface,...>]
#
# Backends: go python cpp javascript rust docs. Omit the list for all of them.
#
# A surface is one declaration the definition makes — a struct, an enum, a
# constant, the vocabulary, the protocol. `-only=` names every surface the
# artefact carries, so an adopter of the envelope alone does not receive the
# record codec. The list is the whole contents and not a set of roots: the
# generator refuses one that does not close, and its refusal names both what is
# missing and what needed it. Omit the flag for the whole definition.
#
# This is a declaration and not an allow-list. An allow-list removes an
# obligation — a line is added and a check stops firing. Every line here adds
# one: scripts/check.sh regenerates what the line names and refuses any
# difference, refuses a line whose definition is gone, and refuses a generated
# file committed under an output root no line declares. Leaving a line out
# hides nothing; it turns the artefact red instead.
#
# A definition that feeds no committed artefact needs no line. Nothing can be
# stale about a file that does not exist.

job/job.thrift	site	docs
job/job.thrift	job	go	-only=Step,Progress,Recall,Lease,Delegation,Intent,Envelope,Record,Request,Response,Verdict,Content,Store

# The job definition feeds Go and the page, and no line here gives it a Python
# or a C++ artefact — not because those backends cannot emit what the job layer
# declares, but because no program can hold two of their artefacts at once. Each
# lands at a fixed identity: package `py` for Python, `namespace rec` for C++,
# sixteen of whose names both copies define. The conformance drivers under
# download/ already carry the download layer's copy, so a second one is shadowed
# in Python and a redefinition in C++, and the Verdict transcript tokens they
# print are spelled by hand there and diffed against Go's. Giving the artefact a
# name from its definition would fix it and rewrites every `rec::` in the tree.

# One definition, three roots, because each backend's own relative path lands
# where that language already looks: a Go package under the module, a header
# under the include root the CMake install copies, and a directory beside the
# Python module. The Python artefact is generated and NOT in the wheel —
# `py-modules = ["abstraction_download"]` ships one file, and the only way to
# add `py/rec.py` to that distribution is to claim the top-level import name
# `py`, which belongs to somebody else on PyPI. It is the authority the shipped
# module is tested against instead; see download/CONTRACT.md § Two endings.
download/download.thrift	download	go
download/download.thrift	download/cpp/include/abstraction/download	cpp
download/download.thrift	download/python	python
